                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/philosophy+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %p This entry is about the article
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
              %p
                %em Some Thoughts on the Future of Category Theory
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/BFb0084208"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0084208
                )
              %p In: A. Carboni, M. Pedicchio, G. Rosolini (ed.s)
              %p
                %em> Category Theory
                ,
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Como"}> Proceedings of the International Conference held in Como
                ,
              %p Lecture Notes in Mathematics 1488, Springer (1991)
          %p
            about the formalization of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+logic"}> objective logic
            ” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"}> Science of Logic
            ) and in particular (implicitly) the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+toposes"} cohesive toposes
          %p
            A closely related text is
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#exegesis"} Exegesis
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#exegesis Exegesis
          %p
            The article is written in a style typical for Lawvere, where precise general abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theoretic
            situations are discussed more in prose than in the usual style of mathematical writing. The thoughts revolve around a topic that Lawvere takes up in various later articles, which are all listed in the References-section at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %em exegesis
            may or may not accurately reflect some of the original author’s actual intentions.
          %p
            Pages 6 to 8 in section II of the text is to a large extent a proposal that there is a useful formalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            and their “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"}> Aufhebung
            ” that governs
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"} Georg Hegel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysics
            as laid out in his
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
            and
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\emptyset \dashv \ast)
            is “pure being” and refinements thereof such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\flat \dashv \sharp)
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
          %p
            Specifically, the notion of a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+being"} category of being
            that Lawvere discusses (following terminology in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel's
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            %em Some Thoughts on the Future of Category Theory
            is the notion that more recently he has been calling a
            %em category of cohesion
            \. The following tries to illuminate a bit what’s going on .
          %p
            We restrict attention to the case that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            “of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} Being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            and say
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            for short. This is a topos that satisfies a small collection of simple but powerful axioms that are supposed to ensure that its
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            built out of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological structure
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/big+topos"} big topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            may take place.
          %p We walk through the main bits of the article:
          %p
            One
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            is that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global section
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma : \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{S}
            to the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            has a further
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo !
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 := \Gamma_! : \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{S}
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma^{\ast}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Disc} := \Gamma^{\ast}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0(X)
            “of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected"} connected
            components”. What Lawvere calls a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topos"} connected object
            in the article (p. 4) is hence one that is sent by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
          %p
            Another
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
            preserves finite
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
            in a second way, a geometric way.
          %p
            The meaning of this, like that of various other aspects of cohesive toposes, may be clearer as we make the evident step to
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            In this more encompassing context the extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
            becomes a left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} adjoint (∞,1)-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_\infty
            which we just write
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid+in+a+locally+infinity-connected+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} geometric fundamental ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concordance"} concordance
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            The next
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            on a cohesive topos says that there is also a further
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo !
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{coDisc} := \Gamma^! : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{E}
            to a total
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+quadruple"} adjoint quadruple
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Pi_0 \dashv \mathrm{Disc} \dashv \Gamma \dashv \mathrm{coDisc}) :=
                  (\Gamma_! \dashv \Gamma^* \dashv \Gamma_* \dashv \Gamma^!) : \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{S}
          %p
            such that both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Disc}
            as well as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{coDisc}
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
          %p
            This is what Lawvere is talking about from the bottom of p. 6 on. The
            %em downward functor
            that he mentions is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma : \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{S}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Disc}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{coDisc}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete cohesion
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+space"} codiscrete (indiscrete) cohesion
            \. For instance in the case that cohesive structure is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
            and the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indiscrete+space"} indiscrete topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Disc}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{coDisc}
            hence make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            in two ways (p. 7), though only the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{coDisc}
            will also be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
          %p
            (This has, by the way, an important implication that Lawvere does not seem to mention: it implies that we are entitled to the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-topos"} quasi-topos
            induced by the sub-topos. That, one can show, may be identified with the collection of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+sheaf"} concrete
            cohesive spaces. In the case of the cohesive topos for differential geometry, the concrete objects in this sense are precisely the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"}> diffeological space
              s
            \. )
          %p
            He calls the subtopos given by the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{coDisc} : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{E}
            that of “pure
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} Becoming
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} Becoming
          %p
            whereas any old
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            is a collection of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"}> space
              s
            , a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            comes with the extra adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            which, as mentioned above, has the interpretation of sending any space to its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            %em geometric paths
            in any cohesive
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            along paths and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            along higher
            = succeed "al" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %em dynamics
            \. In fact there is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            in every cohesive
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            Now, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete object
            there are no non-trivial paths (formally because by the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Disc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            we have
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi Disc \simeq Id
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+space"} codiscrete object
            every sequence of points whatsoever counts as a path, hence the distinction between the space and its “dynamics” disappears and so we have “pure
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
          %p
            Notice next that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            induces an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+monad"} adjoint monad
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} coDisc
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\mathrm{Disc} \;\Gamma \dashv \mathrm{coDisc}\; \Gamma) : \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}
          %p
            This is what Lawvere calls the
            %em skeleton
            and the
            %em coskeleton
            on p. 7. In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            context the left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mo ♭
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\flat} := \mathrm{Disc} \; \Gamma
            has the interpretation of sending any object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to the coefficient for cohomology of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+system"} local system
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            The paragraph wrapping from page 7 to 8 comments on the possibility that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            is not just that of sets.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S} =
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %em super cohesion
            (in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            and
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth super-geometry
            is cohesive over the base topos of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} bare super-sets
          %p
            What follows on page 9 are thoughts which it seems Lawvere has not formalized further later on. But then on the bottom of p. 9 he gets to the axiomatic identification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            or
            %em formal
            spaces in the cohesive topos. In the more recent article
            %em Axiomatic Cohesion
            what he says here on p. 9 is formalized as follows: he says an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{E}
            is infinitesimal if the canonical morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma X \to \Pi_0 X
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 X = *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is connected. Then the isomorphism condition means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has exactly one global point. But
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be bigger: it may be a formal neighbourhood of that point, for instance it may be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} infinitesimally thickened point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Spec
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Spec} \;k[x]/(x^2)
            that is formally dual to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+dual+numbers"} ring of dual numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma X \to \Pi_0 X
            is an iso is hence a disjoint union of formal neighbourhoods of points.
          %p
            Again, the meaning of this becomes more pronounced in the context of
            = succeed "es:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo *
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Π
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma X \simeq * \simeq \Pi X
            have the interpretation of being
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"}> formal ∞-groupoid
              s
            , for instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} formally exponentiated
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+theory"} ∞-Lie theory
            canonically in every cohesive
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            More discussion of all this is at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %p
            18 years later at the same place, Lawvere gives a lecture series on this topic:
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+--+Combinatorial+and+Infinitesimal+Cases"} Cohesive Toposes – Combinatorial and Infinitesimal Cases
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              A sequence of videos covering the talk in Como is available
              %a{:href => "http://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLRIsuk3ZOitzDqeVKOZhe0MrzEUtRNAh5"} on YouTube
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/reference"} reference
